and mr lynch went on to um say that he  was hoping for coordinated action from  other unions  as well um during the summer uh here in  the united kingdom pat mcfadden is uh  with us now hello to mr mcfadden is that  something that labour would support  no it's not we  very much regret that it's come to this  strike it will cause  enormous  inconvenience to the public as you've  been reporting on your program  today we've got this strike today others  scheduled later in the week and perhaps  the bigger threat of ongoing industrial  disputes through the summer  no one wants to see this  we want to see this brought to an end as  quickly as possible with a deal that  allows the railways to work  and the traveling public to get to work  and all the other uses that people make  of the railway system  yeah but my question is would labor  support coordinated action by other  unions to support the rmt and also to  further their cause  no i answered we we don't want to see  that we want to see these things settled  by negotiation we understand why people  are pressing for  pay rises it's only a few months since  the prime minister stood up at the tory  conference and said that he supported a  high-wage economy that we were breaking  with the old model of a low-wage economy  and now we've got inflation  uh touching 10 so we understand why  people are pressing for pay rises but  it's far better  for these things to be resolved in a  proper way  in a negotiation  rather than the  huge inconvenience to the public that's  going to be caused by this dispute today  okay just let me ask you one more time  would labor support coordinated action  by other unions  no we don't want to see  lots of industrial disputes we want  these things to be resolved by  negotiation i hope i've been clear on  that okay  for the third time  okay let me ask you about uh a  shadow uh minister on a picket line this  morning is that something that you would  do  uh it's not something i'll be doing  today what i'll be doing today is using  every opportunity that i can to  urge a negotiated settlement i don't  want to see the government use this  strike as some kind of divisive wedge  issue of the week  i think the public deserves better than  that given the inconvenience  that they're going through today and  also given the prospect of further  industrial action in other sectors  ministers can't stand back and view this  as a political opportunity instead they  have to view it as a responsibility  particularly given the prime minister's  pledge for a high-wage economy that he  made just a few months ago  a shadow minister on the picket line is  it something you support if it's not  something that you would do  no i i don't think that's the way that  this will be resolved i think it will be  resolved by the union the employers and  hopefully the government being involved  in reaching a deal that allows the  railways uh to resume operation and  that's what i want to see as quickly as  possible  do you expect her to be disciplined by  the leader given that apparently uh he  had uh emailed to say that people should  your members should  the shadow cabinet and shadow minister  should not be taking part uh in this  industrial action by supporting people  on the picket line  well that's a matter for the wips and  for kier stammer it's not something i  would be involved in  okay  chatting to both uh both sides of the  dispute today when i say both sides i  mean the government and the union and  and both pretty incalcerant uh neither  uh looks as though they are going to be  getting around the table again anytime  soon in fact uh we heard uh from uh the  uh government in the form of grant chaps  and he was saying um it would actually  make matters worse if he got involved  yeah i heard him say that and  uh i think that maybe what's changed  from the past  uh is the attitude of government overall  to this you know we've had a lot of talk  in recent days about the 1970s and there  are  uh you know echoes of this we're having  to wait a lot longer for things we're  having to wait for passports for driving  licenses for gp appointments uh people  are waiting longer at the airport and so  on now you've got these industrial  disputes it seems like  we've got a government that's losing its  grip  issue by issue that's what's different  to the recent past things that we used  to take for granted are taking so much  longer and the government seems to have  no idea how to fix these problems we're  living in very much a backlog britain  right now and we've got to get back to  having a government with a grip that's  able to sort these things out  yeah to be fair the winter discontent  was a labor government  [Music]  40 years ago it was and we paid a very  big political price  about that and if i could act as an  advisor to the government right now they  might not want my advice  but if they want to learn from that they  might learn that standing back and  watching this unfold and not trying to  get a grip of it  may not end happily for them because  whoever is responsible for this dispute  is certainly not the party that's been  in opposition for the last 12 years  so grant shap said it's not his job to  get involved with the negotiations why  is he wrong  i just don't think that will hold if  we've got a prospect not just of uh  several strikes this week on the  railways but possibly that railway  strike spinning on into  the summer and disputes in other areas  too is he going to appear or  he and fellow ministers going to appear  on your program for weeks on end saying  it's nothing to do with us i think we  need a government again  not just a permanent political campaign  that sees every issue that comes along  as an opportunity to divide people and  sadly  that's been the approach to this dispute  and the approach to so many issues which  are arising week to week right now  how concerned are you that we are  heading towards the summer of discontent  i think it's worrying  if you look at the newspapers this  morning you see  that of course the railways are front  and center of what we're talking about  but they're not the only issue where  there are pay pressures those pay  pressures are understandable given the  inflation figures of course people are  going to  try to campaign  for a decent pay rise  but what we don't want to see is a  summer of discontent  a series of industrial disputes that  will be bad for the public i want to see  these things properly resolved through  collective bargaining through  negotiation  through the mechanisms that are there  that's going to be best for the country  rather than  this dispute today being replicated in  other sectors  but but what else can the government do  you know they can't accordingly even  according to the governor of the bank of  england they cannot put in place uh  inflation busting pay rises uh because  otherwise we will continue to spiral as  far as our inflation issues are  concerned do you have at least some  sympathy for the government  look i understand the position but we've  got a short-term problem and a long-term  problem as usual the short-term problem  is resolving these disputes and helping  people with the cost of living crisis  right now but this didn't happen  overnight the reason that we've got such  pay pressure is because we've had a  decade of stagnating pay and the reason  we've had a decade of stagnating pay is  we've had very poor economic growth in  this country for the last 10 years let  me give you an example if we'd continued  with the kind of economic growth and the  growth in pay that we had in the first  decade of this century when my party  were in power wages would be on average  11 000 pounds a year higher right now  that would be an entirely different set  of circumstances so what we the reason  that pay is under such pressure at the  moment is because of the long economic  record of poor growth that we've seen  under 